      },
      "description": "In .NET 8, the default OpenAPI library was `Swashbuckle.AspNetCore` (see the csproj).\r\n\r\nAdding OpenAPI support to ASP.NET Core is quite simple!\r\n\r\nWe need to add some services to the DI container and then map OpenAPI and SwaggerUI endpoints."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/OpenApiInDotnet8/Controllers/TalksController.cs",
      "line": 41,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 36,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 47,
          "character": 75
        }
      },
      "description": "Some demo's use Minimal API's. Some use controllers. This one (which is still .NET 8) features controllers.\r\n\r\nThis is the endpoint which creates a talk. You can see XML comments, which helps decorate the summary, parameter and description for OpenAPI.\r\n\r\nThis endpoint is a POST, and it can do multiple things. `ProducesResponseType` tells the OpenAPI document about each of them:\r\n\r\n- It can return 200 OK with the created talk.\r\n- It can return 400 Bad Request when validation fails.\r\n- It can also return 409 Conflict if the talk title already exists.\r\n\r\n`ProducesDefaultResponseType` is quite a cool attribute. It tells the OpenAPI spec that any undocumented status code returns `Problem Details`, the standard for API error reporting.\r\n\r\nFinally, I want to call some attention to setting response descriptions in .NET 8. You can see a `response` tag here, which sets the code (`409`) with the response description.\r\n\r\nHowever, this `response` doesn't show up in autocomplete, and you need to keep your XML in sync with the attribute."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/OpenApiInDotnet8/openapi.json",
      "line": 312,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 308,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 313,
          "character": 13
        }
      },
      "description": "This is the OpenAPI document that Swashbuckle generates.\r\n\r\nEvery operation has the \"default\" case I've talked about (for example on line 44). Any status codes that are returned will have the mentioned response body, which is Problem Details.\r\n\r\nAnd this is the talk model. You can see the required properties, and then each property being defined with a type, description and nullability.\r\n\r\nFun fact: You can see Swashbuckle making a small \"mistake\". It sets `Title` up as nullable, even though we used C#'s `required` keyword AND marked it as `string`, which is non-nullable. I believe NSwag does do this correctly 😉.\r\n\r\n**DO:**\r\n\r\n- Go to http://localhost:5300/swagger to show off SwaggerUI quickly"
    }
  ]
